Isotopologues are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of atoms, but have the same connectivity. For example, \(^{12}C\) and \(^{13}C\) isotopologues of methane (\(CH_4\)) are \(CH_4\) and \(CH_3^{13}C\), respectively. Both are methane molecules, but \(CH_3^{13}C\) contains one \(^{13}C\) atom instead of \(^{12}C\).  

Isotopomers are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of atoms and also differ in the connectivity of atoms. For instance, \(CH_3^{13}C\) and \(CH_2^{13}C-H\) are not isotopologues because their connectivity differs (e.g., \(CH_3^{13}C\) has a terminal carbon, while \(CH_2^{13}C-H\) has a central carbon).  

Thus, isotopologues are a subset of isobaric molecules (molecules with the same mass), while isobaric molecules can also be isobaric molecules that are not isotopologues.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isopologues include molecules with the same connectivity but different isotopic composition.} \\
&\text{Isotpospologues are a subset of isobaric molecules (same mass).}
\end{aligned}
}

